  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale:\n   - We will use a multi-resolution strategy to find an improved solution by testing different numbers of points (n)\n   - The core idea is that a higher resolution allows for a more detailed function which may yield a lower C_5 value by reducing the maximum overlap\n   - While the parent solution uses 300 points, we will systematically test larger multiples (e.g., 600, 900, 1200 points)\n   - This addresses the limitation of local optimal solutions at specific resolutions and leverages the flexibility to better represent h(x)\n   - Key hypotheses: (1) Higher resolution improves configuration freedom, (2) Multi-phase optimization is more powerful for detailed functions\n\n2. Mathematical specification:\n   - Representation: n-point step function samples across [0,2]\n   - Objective: minimize C_5 = max_k \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx (convolution mode='full')\n   - Constraints: 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i, \u222bh(x)dx = 1\n   - Free variable transformation: h = 1 - t for the complement function\n   - Use weighted averaging (0.7 parent + 0.3 random) for initializing higher resolutions\n   - Introduce resolution range [400, 900] to avoid resource waste\n\n3. Implementation procedure:\n   - Maintain three parallel populations: 300, 600, and 900 points\n   - Projection from lower to higher resolution:\n     * Linearly sample parent solution to new resolution\n     * Apply component-wise smoothing (3-tap Gaussian filter)\n     * Scale in/out based on target integral 1\n   - Fusion mechanism across phases:\n     * For each resolution, optimize using multiple phases:\n        - L-BFGS-B with line-search (maximum 50 iterations)\n        - SLSQP with adaptive alpha modification\n   - Budget allocation:\n     * 300-point optimization gets 40% of budget\n     * Subsequent phases encourage resolution hopping\n     * Use sample diversity balancing (neighborhood swaps)\n\n4. Search choices and variations:\n   - Primary resolution set: 300, 400, 500, 600, 750, 900\n   - Use weighted interpolation with parent initialization\n   - Core optimization strategy:\n     * L-BFGS-B primary phase\n     * SLSQP secondary phase with alpha range [1e5, 5e5]\n     * Gradient descent (optional)\n     * Pair search fallback (distance swaps)\n   - Dynamic bounds handling: [0,1] with adaptive padding\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness:\n   - Time allocation: 1100 seconds with adaptive time-based phase termination\n   - Memory: 4-5 million floating point operations per resolution\n   - Safeguards:\n     * Clipping invalid solutions\n     * Resolution fallback if >300 repeats\n     * Weighted objective balancing\n   - Sample validity checks:\n     * Integral must be within 1 \u00b1 1e-5\n     * All components between 0 and 1\n\n6. Validation and return contract:\n   - Final solution must be re-evaluated with complete correlation\n   - Compute time invested for profile accounting\n   - Save best solution at each milestone\n   - Boundary condition handling:\n     * Convolution edge effects\n     * Non-uniform resolution handling\n   - Output format: (h_values, c5_value, n_points)",
